{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "Prototypes are throwaway by definition, so the only thing that matters is whether the idea works; reading every line would waste the speed that makes AI useful. Accept the code, learn from the result, and delete it."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo shown to stakeholders or investors only needs to work on the happy path, but embarrassing crashes or obviously broken behavior can sink the pitch. Let AI write the code, skim it selectively, and rely on quick behavioral tests to confirm the demo holds up."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Real early users mean real data and real trust, and you will have to debug and change this code under pressure. Review every AI change line by line so you understand what is running and can fix it when users hit edge cases."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and a maintaining team require code that everyone can understand, own, and evolve safely over time. Let AI suggest and draft, but a person should write or rewrite every line so the team owns the design and the debt."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 0,
      "rationale": "In regulated or safety-critical systems, correctness, traceability, and accountability cannot be delegated to a model that may be confidently wrong. No AI-written code should enter the system; humans author and verify everything against formal review and audit requirements."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Vibe coding is a dial you turn down as the cost of being wrong goes up. Use full autonomy (4) only for throwaway prototypes, tighten to selective review with tests (3) for demos, require line-by-line review (2) once real users depend on the code, let AI assist but not author in maintained production (1), and keep AI out of critical systems entirely (0). The key is matching the level of unreviewed trust to the blast radius of a defect."
}
